<h2>3. What data is processed and where does it come from?</h2>
<p>We process data that you provide as part of your application and that arises while handling it. This includes, in particular, your name, contact details (such as email address, telephone number and postal address), cover letter, CV, information about your education, qualifications and previous employment, certificates and, where applicable, interview notes necessary for the selection process.</p>
<p>We generally receive this information directly from you. According to our current information, the application sections of our website prepare an email in your own email program; your entries are not submitted as an application form through our web server or stored in an applicant database on the website. Your application is transmitted to us only when you send the email.</p>

<h2>4. Purposes and legal bases of processing</h2>
<p>We process your application data to receive your application, assess your suitability for an advertised position, clarify questions, conduct interviews and decide whether to enter into an employment relationship. Processing is generally based on Article 6(1)(b) GDPR (pre-contractual steps at your request).</p>
<p>Where data is required to fulfil legal obligations, Article 6(1)(c) GDPR applies. Further retention for a limited period following rejection may be based on Article 6(1)(f) GDPR for the establishment or defence of legal claims; our legitimate interest is in maintaining evidence and defending legal claims, including potential claims under the German General Equal Treatment Act (AGG).</p>
<p>According to the current information, we do not intend to use your application for other positions or retain it in an applicant pool for a longer period. Should such processing be introduced, we will inform you beforehand and obtain voluntary consent where necessary.</p>

<h2>5. Special categories of personal data</h2>
<p>Please provide information about your health or other specially protected information only where necessary for the recruitment process. If, for example, you inform us of a severe disability, we process this information only where necessary to exercise rights or fulfil obligations under employment or social security law (Article 9(2)(b) GDPR in conjunction with the applicable statutory provisions). We do not take unnecessary special-category information into account when selecting applicants and delete it where possible.</p>

<h2>6. Who receives your data and how is it processed?</h2>
<p>Incoming application emails are received and stored through Microsoft 365 / Exchange Online. The responsible human resources staff manage them in an Outlook folder accessible only to authorised persons. Other decision-makers involved in the selection process receive the documents only where necessary to decide on your application.</p>
<p>We use Microsoft 365 / Exchange Online to provide and process email communication. The service provider is Microsoft Ireland Operations Limited, Ireland, potentially involving affiliated companies and other subprocessors. Microsoft processes data under the applicable contractual and data protection provisions. Otherwise, access is given only to persons or bodies where legally permissible and necessary for the recruitment process.</p>

<h2>7. Transfers to third countries</h2>
<p>When Microsoft 365 / Exchange Online is used, personal data may be processed outside the European Union and the European Economic Area, particularly in the USA, as part of service provision, especially for support, security or subcontracted services. Where such transfers take place, the requirements of Articles 44 et seq. GDPR must be met. These may include an adequacy decision by the European Commission for appropriately certified recipients or appropriate safeguards, particularly EU standard contractual clauses. Information about the safeguards applicable to a specific transfer is available through the contact details above.</p>

<h2>8. Retention and deletion</h2>
<p>If no employment relationship is established, we generally retain your application documents for six months after completion of the recruitment process. This limited retention period serves, in particular, to enable us to examine and defend any claims that may be asserted. We then delete application data from the active storage locations used for this purpose, unless another legal basis or a statutory obligation requires longer retention in an individual case. Technically necessary backup copies are removed in accordance with the scheduled backup and deletion cycles and are not used for further applicant selection.</p>
<p>If you are hired, we transfer to your personnel file only the information necessary to establish and carry out the employment relationship. This information is then subject to the applicable statutory and internal retention periods.</p>

<h2>9. Provision of data and automated decisions</h2>
<p>There is no statutory obligation to submit an application. However, to assess your application, we need the information that enables us to contact you and evaluate your suitability. Without sufficient information, we may be unable to consider your application.</p>
<p>No solely automated decision-making, including profiling within the meaning of Article 22 GDPR, takes place during the recruitment process.</p>

<h2>10. Your rights</h2>
<p>Subject to the statutory requirements, you have the following rights in particular:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Access to information about the processing of your personal data (Article 15 GDPR),</li>
  <li>Rectification of inaccurate data and completion of incomplete data (Article 16 GDPR),</li>
  <li>Erasure (Article 17 GDPR) and restriction of processing (Article 18 GDPR),</li>
  <li>Data portability, where the statutory requirements are met (Article 20 GDPR),</li>
  <li>Objection on grounds relating to your particular situation to processing under Article 6(1)(f) GDPR (Article 21 GDPR),</li>
  <li>Withdrawal of consent voluntarily given, with effect for the future (Article 7(3) GDPR).</li>
</ul>
<p>The lawfulness of processing carried out before withdrawal remains unaffected. To exercise your rights, you can contact the controller or the data protection officer.</p>

<h2>11. Right to lodge a complaint</h2>
<p>Under Article 77 GDPR, you have the right to lodge a complaint with a data protection supervisory authority. The authority responsible for our company is, in particular:</p>
<p>Der Landesbeauftragte für den Datenschutz und die Informationsfreiheit Baden-Württemberg<br>Heilbronner Straße 35, 70191 Stuttgart<br>Phone: +49 711 615541-0<br>Email: poststelle@lfdi.bwl.de<br>Website: www.baden-wuerttemberg.datenschutz.de</p>

